{"task_id": "Mbpp/286", "solution": "def max_sub_array_sum_repeated(array, length, k):\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the largest sum of a contiguous subarray in the modified array\n    which is formed by repeating the given array k times.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def kadane(arr_input):\n        \"\"\"\n        Calculates the maximum contiguous subarray sum for a given array using Kadane's algorithm.\n        Handles arrays with all negative numbers by returning the largest negative number.\n        Returns 0 for an empty array.\n        \"\"\"\n        if not arr_input:\n            return 0\n        \n        max_so_far = arr_input[0]\n        current_max = arr_input[0]\n        \n        for i in range(1, len(arr_input)):\n            current_max = max(arr_input[i], current_max + arr_input[i])\n            max_so_far = max(max_so_far, current_max)\n            \n        return max_so_far\n\n    # Handle edge cases for empty array or zero repetitions.\n    if not array or length == 0 or k == 0:\n        return 0\n\n    # Case 1: If the array is repeated only once, apply Kadane's directly.\n    if k == 1:\n        return kadane(array)\n\n    # Calculate the maximum subarray sum within a single copy of the original array.\n    max_sum_single_copy = kadane(array)\n\n    # Calculate the sum of all elements in the original array.\n    total_array_sum = sum(array)\n\n    # Calculate the maximum subarray sum within two concatenated copies of the original array.\n    # This covers cases where the maximum subarray spans across the boundary of two copies.\n    max_sum_double_copy = kadane(array + array)\n\n    # Case 2: If the total sum of the original array is positive.\n    # In this scenario, repeating the array k times allows us to potentially gain more sum\n    # by including (k-2) full positive sum arrays in the middle, combined with the maximal\n    # spanning subarray found in two copies.\n    if total_array_sum > 0:\n        # The result is the maximum of:\n        # 1. Max sum within a single copy (max_sum_single_copy).\n        # 2. Max sum spanning up to two copies (max_sum_double_copy).\n        # 3. Max sum spanning multiple copies: This is achieved by taking the max sum found\n        #    in two copies (which includes the best prefix/suffix combination) and adding\n        #    the sum of the remaining (k-2) full arrays.\n        return max(max_sum_single_copy, max_sum_double_copy, max_sum_double_copy + (k - 2) * total_array_sum)\n    else:\n        # Case 3: If the total sum of the original array is zero or negative.\n        # Adding more full arrays will not increase the maximum sum (it will either stay\n        # the same or decrease). Therefore, the maximum subarray sum will be found\n        # either within a single copy or spanning at most two copies.\n        return max(max_sum_single_copy, max_sum_double_copy)"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/296", "solution": "```python\ndef get_Inv_Count(arr: list[int]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Counts the number of inversions in the given array.\n    An inversion occurs when a larger element appears before a smaller element.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(arr)\n    inv_count = 0\n    for i in range(n):\n        for j in range(i + 1, n):\n            if arr[i] > arr[j]:\n                inv_count += 1\n    return inv_count\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/294", "solution": "def _is_numeric(value):\n    \"\"\"Checks if a given value is an integer or a float.\"\"\"\n    return isinstance(value, (int, float))\n\ndef _filter_numeric_values(input_list):\n    \"\"\"Filters a list to return only numeric (int or float) values.\"\"\"\n    return [item for item in input_list if _is_numeric(item)]\n\ndef max_val(input_list):\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the maximum numeric value in a heterogeneous list, ignoring non-numeric elements.\n    Raises ValueError if no numeric values are found.\n    \"\"\"\n    numeric_values = _filter_numeric_values(input_list)\n    if not numeric_values:\n        raise ValueError(\"No numeric values found in the list.\")\n    return max(numeric_values)"}
