{"task_id": "Mbpp/603", "solution": "from typing import List\n\ndef get_ludic(n: int) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Generate all lucid numbers less than or equal to n.\n    Returns an ordered list of lucid numbers <= n.\n    \"\"\"\n    if n <= 0:\n        return []\n    \n    def _generate_candidates(limit: int) -> List[int]:\n        \"\"\"Generates an initial list of candidate numbers from 1 to limit.\"\"\"\n        return list(range(1, limit + 1))\n\n    def _apply_ludic_sieve(candidates: List[int]) -> List[int]:\n        \"\"\"Applies the ludic sieve algorithm to a list of candidates.\"\"\"\n        ludic_numbers = []\n        current_candidates = list(candidates)\n\n        while current_candidates:\n            # The first element remaining is always the next lucid number\n            L = current_candidates.pop(0)\n            ludic_numbers.append(L)\n\n            if not current_candidates:\n                break\n            \n            # Remove every L-th element from the remaining candidates.\n            # L=1 doesn't remove anything beyond itself. Sieve starts effectively with L=2.\n            if L > 1:\n                next_candidates_after_sieve = []\n                count = 0\n                for x in current_candidates:\n                    count += 1\n                    if count % L != 0:\n                        next_candidates_after_sieve.append(x)\n                current_candidates = next_candidates_after_sieve\n        \n        return ludic_numbers\n\n    initial_candidates = _generate_candidates(n)\n    return _apply_ludic_sieve(initial_candidates)"}
