{"task_id": "Mbpp/785", "solution": "def tuple_str_int(input_string: str) -> tuple:\n    \"\"\"\n    Converts a string representation of a tuple into an actual Python tuple of integers.\n    Assumes the input string starts with '(' and ends with ')'.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Remove outer parentheses from the input string.\n    content = input_string[1:-1]\n\n    # If the content is empty or contains only whitespace, return an empty tuple.\n    if not content.strip():\n        return ()\n\n    # Split the content by comma to get string representations of elements.\n    elements_str = content.split(',')\n    \n    int_elements = []\n    for s in elements_str:\n        # Strip whitespace from each element string.\n        stripped_s = s.strip()\n        # Only attempt to convert non-empty strings to integers.\n        # This handles cases like \"(7,)\" or \"( , 8)\" by ignoring empty parts.\n        if stripped_s:\n            int_elements.append(int(stripped_s))\n            \n    # Convert the list of integers into a tuple.\n    return tuple(int_elements)"}
